AI Use Level 3: Open AI Use
The goal of this policy is to openly allow students to use generative AI tools in their learning. For example, in this course, generative AI tools could be used for
* brainstorming and developing ideas that might form the seed for a paper, project, or presentation;
* seeking alternative explanations for course content to help you understand the material;
* generating text, code, images, videos, and other media that can be used, in whole or in part, in the final submission;
* summarizing research sources or complex topics to aid in understanding;
* revising writing to improve clarity, grammar, or style;
* checking your work for errors or getting help to locate the source of an error or misunderstanding;
* translating text from one language to another.

Please keep in mind that AI tools aren’t reliable sources of information, so you will need to be careful when relying on its outputs in any context—just because you are permitted to use AI doesn’t mean it is the best tool for the job.

If generative AI tools play a significant role in the genesis of your work, they should be credited in the same way that you would share authorship with a collaborating lab partner in a publication.

